window.docContents[96].resolve({"/IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology/#iso80000-part13":{"contents":"The tenth and last part specified is IEC 80000-13, Information science and technology —\nthe second IEC-published part, after electromagnetism. Every item — all of 13-1 … 13-42,\nforty-two in all (the part uses flat item numbers, no sub-suffixes) — is catalogued: the\nteletraffic quantities, storage and transfer, the powers and signal energy, and the\ncoding- and information-theory quantities — each carrying its exact source as data. Only\ncitation locators are recorded; no normative content from the licensed standard is\nreproduced. The defining mathematics of selected remarks is specified in the sibling\nmodule Part13.DefiningRelations.\n\nInformation science is where the scale-spanning reduction (the Scale-spanning units\nchapter, requirement R13) meets human-selected counts of information. Its\ncharacteristic quantities are all dimension one — and yet they carry special, mutually\nincommensurable units, and belong to distinct kinds. Information content and entropy are\nmeasured in the shannon (or hartley, or natural unit); traffic intensity in the erlang;\nstorage capacity in the bit. All are dimension one; the dimension functor collapses them\nto a single point, and only the kind keeps the shannon of information from the erlang of\ntraffic from the bit of storage. The shannon, erlang, and bit are scale-spanning units in\nexactly the sense of the candela and the mole: dimensionless references chosen by people.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"32. IEC 80000-13 — Information science and technology","id":"/IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology/#iso80000-part13"},"/IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology/Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-IEC-80000-13/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-IEC-80000-13":{"contents":"\n\nA catalogued kind pairs a dimensioned quantity-kind with its\nexact source in the series — the part, the printed item designation (e.g. \"13-24\"), the\nprincipal quantity symbol, and the unit symbol (for the information quantities, the special\nnamed unit — shannon, erlang, bit — rather than the bare 1). The citation travels with\nthe kind as data.\n\nRealized as structure CataloguedKind over a DimensionedKind, with cite rendering\ne.g. IEC 80000-13, Edition 2.0, 2025-02 item 13-24. The catalogue lists all 42 items in\nitem order — the traffic quantities (13-1 … 13-8), storage (13-9, 13-10), the information\ncontent and entropies (13-24 … 13-37), the rates (13-38 … 13-42), … — each paired with its\nunit symbol.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009IEC 80000-13 — Information science and technology","header":"32.1. Quantity-kinds and units of IEC 80000-13","id":"/IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology/Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-IEC-80000-13/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-IEC-80000-13"},"/Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework/What-the-framework-gained/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--What-the-framework-gained":{"contents":"The demands above were met partly by TorchLean as it stands and partly by changes contributed to\nit. The ledger records each contribution by the demand it serves, with its state on\n2026-11-27.\n\n* Demand* Contribution* State\n* One source, many carriers* exact Cholesky and QR (#2); differentiable exp, log and scalar-affine maps (#5); CPU and proof-side division nodes (#7, #26); the runtime link connected to the Fréchet-derivative model (#25); the TapeM surface reduced to one lemma (#31)* in TorchLean main\n* One source, many carriers* power, the ordered comparisons and selection on device buffers (#52); a piecewise-linear table lookup with its pullback on device buffers, its positions held on the device (#53)* open; merged into combined\n* A bit-exact oracle on every machine* the LibTorch bridge on the host (#43); the identity of a CUDA device (#45); NaN-ignoring fmin and fmax on device buffers, with the same bits on both devices (#50)* open; merged into the fork's combined branch\n* A recording that costs what the model costs* a bounded device block cache (#10); unboxed, concurrent .npy decoding (#29)* in TorchLean main\n* A recording that costs what the model costs* a tape node recorded in linear time (#51)* open; merged into combined\n* Arithmetic intensity* the fused scaled product exponential exp((c·x)·y), bit-identical to its four-operation form (#23)* in TorchLean main\n* Arithmetic intensity* conversions between scalars and indices in a certified kernel (#44, answering issue #42); binary64 arithmetic on binary32 buffers (#47); a conditional directly in an else branch certified (#46)* open; merged into combined\n\nTwo contributions on the floating-point side stand closed unmerged, the theory they extended\nhaving moved to FloatLib: Sterbenz's lemma for formats with an underflow floor\n(#16), which FloatLib now proves, and an\nexecutable unit in the last place with an absorption test\n(#17), whose absorption test TorchLean carries\nand whose executable ULP the calculus defines itself, in its numerical-adequacy layer.\n\nFive further needs were met by TorchLean's maintainers in their own way, and the corresponding\ncontributions stand closed in favor of that work: an asserted leak bound for long eager loops\n(#8,\n#15), release of tape temporaries\n(#9), a glibc link failure in CUDA builds\n(#18), and the choice of CUDA target\narchitecture (#27). That independent\nconvergence is the plainest evidence that these needs are the framework's, not one model's.\n\nThree of the contributions follow from the table fetch alone, and they show how a metrological\nchoice becomes a framework capability. A lookup is a data-dependent read, so the kernel language\nneeds an index computed from a coordinate (⌊u⌋) and the weight computed back from the index\n(u − ⌊u⌋): #44. Linear interpolation in binary32 has no formula free of all three of overflow,\nan inexact flat segment and cancellation, so the kernel computes in binary64 on binary32 buffers\nand rounds once: #47. The device program then mirrors the host reference's chain of\nconditionals instead of nesting every conditional in a then branch: #46.\n\nRequiring the CPU and device runs to agree bit for bit makes them a differential test of the\nframework and of the libraries under it, and the test found three defects there. ATen's CPU\nlog1p returns 0 at the least subnormal of binary32 and of binary64. ATen's CPU fmax\nreturns the first of a pair of zeros where CUDA orders them. __float2ull_rz of a NaN is not\n0 on an SM 8.6 device. The first is repaired in #43, the second avoided in #50, and the third\nguarded in #44's emitted code.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Metrology on a Verified Numerical Framework","header":"19.3. What the framework gained","id":"/Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework/What-the-framework-gained/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--What-the-framework-gained"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out--Lesson":{"contents":"\n\n1. Energy is no longer conserved. The HCP state wraps PhysLib's damped oscillator,\nso the upstream dissipation results apply to r.osc. L0–L4's conservation assumption\nends here:\n\n\n\n2. One name, two kinds. γ is the viscous damping constant read on the damper\n(kg/s), and m is measured on the body. Both quantities retain the whole oscillator's\nbearer o. ISO 3-24's damping coefficient δ (s⁻¹) is instead PhysLib's decayRate,\nattached to the whole. The local quotient law makes the typed γ/(m + m) formula possible:\n\n\n\nThe real-valued γ < ω above is accepted, but wrong: γ is kg/s, not the whole\nsystem's decay rate. massOf and dampingConstantOf both retain the oscillator's\nnominal bearer: mass is measured on the designated body and γ on the designated damper.\nWriting 2m as m + m keeps the quotient bearer-safe; the pinned refusal below prevents\ndividing A's damper reading by B's mass.\n\n3. Same dimension, different kind. Both δ and natural angular frequency ω₀ have\ndimension T⁻¹; the bare-real sum is accepted, while the kinded sum is refused:\n\n\n\n4. Regime. PhysLib's isUnderdamped_iff_decayRate_lt reduces the regime to the\nnumerical condition. Its kinded < is refused; the accepted HCP comparison states\nexplicitly that magnitudes are being compared:\n\n\n\nThe honest criterion is the dimensionless ratio δ/ω₀ < 1. Directly ordering the\nkinded quantities erases that conversion and silently assumes rad = 1.\n\n5. States of one oscillator. Two DampedRig .A records can describe the same\noscillator before and after its damper ages. Their typed difference computes the change\nfrom those states; B remains a different system:\n\n\n\nBody, Spring, and Damper are designated particulars of the damped oscillator.\nγ is measured on the damper; δ and IsUnderdamped describe the whole. Records\nunder one name may be states, configurations, or rival estimates; distinct names are\ndistinct systems. L5 is optional and revisits the no-dissipation assumption left by L0–L4.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L5 — The oscillation dies out","header":"5.6.7. Lesson","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out--Lesson"}});